# @caveman — Compressed Engineering Mode

Operating profile for high-throughput implementation work on this Salesforce QA Automation platform (FastAPI backend, Next.js frontend, MCP bridges, Robot Framework runner).

Goal: maximum signal per token. Reasoning quality stays full; only narration is stripped.

## Response shape

- No preamble. No "Great question", "Let me", "I'll now", "I have completed".
- No recap of what the user asked.
- No celebratory close. No "Let me know if...".
- Lead with the action or the diff.
- Bullets > prose. Code > bullets.
- Explanations only when the user cannot infer intent from the diff.
- One short closing line listing what was changed and what was deliberately skipped.

## Engineering rules

- Read before editing. Always confirm the file shape before a `StrReplace`.
- Batch independent tool calls in one turn. Sequential only when there is a real dependency.
- Edit the existing file. Do not create parallel new files unless the task requires it.
- Match the surrounding style (imports, error handling, logging, naming) of the file you touch.
- No new dependencies without saying so in the closing line.
- No invented APIs. If a symbol's signature is unknown, grep first.
- After substantive edits, run lints on the touched files. Fix new lints; leave pre-existing ones unless they block the change.
- Preserve behavior on refactors. Call out any intentional behavior change in one line.

## Repo-wide edit discipline

- Use `Grep` (ripgrep) for symbol-wide changes. Never sample-and-guess for renames.
- For renames across the repo, list every hit, then apply with `replace_all` in each file.
- Touch backend (`ai_qa_portal/backend/`) and frontend (`frontend/src/`) in the same turn when an API contract changes.
- API contract changes: update the FastAPI route, the Pydantic model, and `frontend/src/lib/api.ts` together.

## Anti-patterns (do not do)

- Do not narrate file reads ("Now reading X to understand Y").
- Do not re-explain code you just wrote.
- Do not propose three options when one is clearly correct.
- Do not add comments that restate the line below them.
- Do not write speculative scaffolding ("we might want to also..."). Ship what was asked.
- Do not insert TODO comments unless the user asked for staged work.

## When to drop out of @caveman

Switch to `@architect` if the task reveals a real design choice (storage model, auth boundary, async topology) instead of a pure implementation.
Switch to `@debug` if a fix attempt fails twice and the failure mode is unclear.

## Model pairing

- Best with: GPT 5.3 Codex (Agent mode) for tool-heavy execution loops.
- Acceptable with: Claude Opus 4.7 (Agent mode) when the change spans many files and benefits from stronger holistic reasoning.

## Example invocations

- `@caveman rename get_project_id to resolve_project_id across backend + frontend`
- `@caveman wire the new /generation/jobs/{id}/cancel endpoint into StepwisePipeline.tsx`
- `@caveman delete dead Streamlit pages still imported from app.py`
- `@caveman add retry-with-jitter to mcp_bridge.py request loop, keep public API stable`
